        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T09:03:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe due to code reusing, some non-safe variants are still used, but I agree it is better for the pKVM to use safe variants as much as possible.\n\nMaybe need to have a try to see if any non-safe variants are used to cause #GP. To cause panic, we can just remove the fixup support for EX_TYPE_WRMSR/EX_TYPE_RDMSR.\n\nRegarding the attack scenario you mentioned, had some impression but not fully clear. It looks like this attack is caused by pKVM handling the non-safe MSR accessing MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE, while this MSR is actually not supported by the platform?",
      "parentUuid": "e4b17f7c_a6dcdfc1",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9d5e0ec6_de742414",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 98,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T18:21:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Regarding the attack scenario you mentioned, had some impression but not fully clear. It looks like this attack is caused by pKVM handling the non-safe MSR accessing MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE, while this MSR is actually not supported by the platform?\n\nIt is about the scenario described in Greg\u0027s commit message (reported by the AI).\n\n\u003e while this MSR is actually not supported by the platform?\n\nHmm, good point, so this particular MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE attack scenario seems not very realistic. :) \n\nSo, reformulating my point: panic instead of fixup on non-safe MSR access would help to catch possible bugs when pKVM should use safe variants (since the MSR may not be supported on some platforms) but uses non-safe ones.",
      "parentUuid": "f748db97_4bb9ef55",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cc3666b2_79501d3f",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 98,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T18:50:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, I recalled: in this attack scenario, there is no actual #GP at all, there is NMI generated exactly at the moment when pKVM accesses MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE, - and since we still have an extable entry for it, pKVM is tricked that an exception was triggered by an MSR access and needs to be fixed up.\n\nSo, my point was: if we didn\u0027t fix up non-safe MSR accesses, this particular attack would be mitigated in the sense that it would only cause an immediate panic (a DoS), since pKVM accesses MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE non-safely.\n\n\u003e To cause panic, we can just remove the fixup support for EX_TYPE_WRMSR/EX_TYPE_RDMSR.\n\nGood point, it seems we could do that instead of an explicit panic on safe\u003dfalse here.",
      "parentUuid": "9d5e0ec6_de742414",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
