      "writtenOn": "2026-07-25T11:33:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just a question: are there already any cases when we fix up #UD, not just #GP?",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3001ba4_2fa7ca30",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-25T11:41:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IIRC we added exception fixup support specifically to support rdmsr_safe/wrmsr_safe for virtualizating host accesses to those MSR that are outside the range configured via MSR_BITMAP and thus can\u0027t be passed-through to the host. Before we discovered that issue, we didn\u0027t need exception fixup. So I suppose there are no cases when we need to fix up #UD?",
      "parentUuid": "1344f7a6_71374e79",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "083f02d7_43464597",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-25T13:40:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I recall we also leverage exception fixup in a couple more cases when KVM code reused by pKVM relies on it, e.g.: 1. rdmsrq_safe/wrmsrq_safe for probing uret MSRs, e.g. in kvm_probe_user_return_msr(). 2. handling spurious failures of VMX instructions in vmx_asm1() etc (that actually always causes pKVM to BUG_ON anyway).\n\nBut in both these cases it is still #GP, not #UD? (VMX instructions only trigger #UD if VMX is disabled, which must never happen in pKVM?)",
      "parentUuid": "e3001ba4_2fa7ca30",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce7c6cc6_bdd429bd",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-25T18:53:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just noticed there is one more case: executing XSETBV instruction in `handle_xsetbv()` (for handling trapped XSETBV instruction executed by the host). But the comment in `handle_xsetbv()` explains that, again, in pKVM it should only generate #GP, not #UD?\n\nAlthough that comment also says that it was not confirmed on older CPUs that don\u0027t have XSAVE. But we probably don\u0027t support such CPUs anyway?",
      "parentUuid": "083f02d7_43464597",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cb7faf97_311763e9",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-27T14:04:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think you are right: currently we have no #UD extable use-cases in pKVM today. But keeping it as is seems safe for future use and has no risk of causing the spurious asynchronous fixup bug (NMI/vector 2) that this patch fixes.\n\nSo I think keeping it as is is ok, or you recommend to remove it for some other reason?",
      "parentUuid": "ce7c6cc6_bdd429bd",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "43823fc2_6d329fae",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-27T14:19:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was thinking it might be worth removing it as a defense-in-depth, for cases when the hypervisor unexpectedly executes an undefined/unsupported instruction due to a bug in pKVM (so that we\u0027d better immediately panic in this case), and a malicious host exploits this bug and somehow manipulates the hypervisor so that it ends up doing this \"fixup\"...",
      "parentUuid": "cb7faf97_311763e9",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "20b64c46_9bcb2c13",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T09:03:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003eBut in both these cases it is still #GP, not #UD? (VMX instructions only trigger #UD if VMX is disabled, which must never happen in pKVM?)\n\nPer SDM, the VMX instruction can trigger #UD even in VMX is enabled. E.g.,\nIF (register operand) or (not in VMX operation) or (CR0.PE \u003d 0) or (RFLAGS.VM \u003d 1) or (IA32_EFER.LMA \u003d 1 and CS.L \u003d 0)\nTHEN #UD;\nAlthough pKVM also doesn\u0027t change these registers.\n\n\u003eI was thinking it might be worth removing it as a defense-in-depth, for cases when the hypervisor unexpectedly executes an undefined/unsupported instruction due to a bug in pKVM (so that we\u0027d better immediately panic in this case), and a malicious host exploits this bug and somehow manipulates the hypervisor so that it ends up doing this \"fixup\"...\n\nFixup doesn\u0027t fix unconditionally. It will check if there is a fixup entry in the exception table. If pKVM executes an instruction that defines a fixup entry in the exception table, most likely it means the #UD caused by this instruction is wanted to be handled. If pKVM executes such an instruction which doesn\u0027t have a fixup entry in the exception table, the fixup will do nothing and pKVM\u0027s #UD handler will run to panic.",
      "parentUuid": "43823fc2_6d329fae",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "96afc921_8cd30ead",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T09:18:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Per SDM, the VMX instruction can trigger #UD even in VMX is enabled. E.g.,\nIF (register operand) or (not in VMX operation) or (CR0.PE \u003d 0) or (RFLAGS.VM \u003d 1) or (IA32_EFER.LMA \u003d 1 and CS.L \u003d 0)\nTHEN #UD;\n\n\nI was assuming that VMX root mode itself requires 64-bit mode. Am I wrong?\n\n\u003e Fixup doesn\u0027t fix unconditionally. It will check if there is a fixup entry in the exception table.\n\nYes, but I was thinking that maybe the host could _somehow_ manipulate pKVM to execute it anyway (using, again, a bug in pKVM).\n\nMaybe this is too paranoid thinking. So if you think it\u0027s still better to keep supporting #UD fixup, I\u0027m ok with that.",
      "parentUuid": "20b64c46_9bcb2c13",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d133e981_93bcca43",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/idt.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 166,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-28T10:19:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I was assuming that VMX root mode itself requires 64-bit mode. Am I wrong?\n\nNo, you are right. Just got what you mean. 😊\n\n\u003eMaybe this is too paranoid thinking. So if you think it\u0027s still better to keep supporting #UD fixup, I\u0027m ok with that.\n\nI am not against removing #UD if it is not used currently, just want to explain that the code will not blindly handle #UD, only the instruction sits in exception table will be handled. Actually I agree that, if there is no user for #UD, maybe removing it for now is better from the patch clean point of view, and add it when it is needed in the future.",
      "parentUuid": "96afc921_8cd30ead",
      "revId": "246398714a0fb05d1c22a45ce0c130c1067aec4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
